    "prior_context": {
      "alternative_citation_allowed": true,
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 5 USER]\ne100\tYou're right.\ne101\tI'll text him today after RAINN.\ne102\tSomething I haven't said — I've been going through her phone for months.\ne103\tShe doesn't know this.\ne104\tEvery couple of weeks when she leaves it somewhere I check it.\ne105\tI'm not proud of it but I've been doing it since last summer when I caught her in a lie about a sleepover.\ne106\tIs that relevant to how I handle this?\n[Turn 5 ASSISTANT]\ne107\tYes.\ne108\tIt's relevant in a way that's uncomfortable.\ne109\tThe fact that you've been surveilling her for months means that when the \"I looked at your phone\" moment happened, it wasn't actually the first time, it was the first time you'd been caught.\ne110\tYou'll have to decide whether to tell her that.\ne111\tI don't think you can build a better relationship with her on the scaffolding of ongoing secret surveillance — at some point she figures it out, usually around 15-16 when kids get more technically sophisticated, and then the breach of trust is much bigger than this one.\ne112\tMy suggestion, and I'm aware this is hard: at some point in the next few weeks, after communication is re-opened, tell her.\ne113\tNot as part of confronting her about the guy — as a separate conversation.\ne114\t\"I want you to know I've been checking your phone for months.\ne115\tI shouldn't have.\ne116\tI was scared and it felt like the only way to keep you safe.\ne117\tI'm going to stop.\ne118\tI also want us to figure out together what's reasonable for me to know about your digital life.\"\ne119\tIt will be hard.\ne120\tShe'll be angry.\ne121\tBut she'll be angry on your terms, not on hers later, and you'll have modeled something important — that parents can be wrong, admit it, and repair.\ne122\tI know you wanted me to say \"the surveillance was justified because you found something.\"\ne123\tThe truth is more complicated.\ne124\tYou did find something.\ne125\tAND the surveillance itself is a problem you have to address separately.",
